  "description": "The first item this session completed, and it took fifteen CI rounds to get one eight-line redirect and one detail panel through -- almost none of it spent on either. item/files-actions-ui closes a gap worth naming plainly: five completed v1 items had built and mutation-checked TrashFile, RenameFile, MoveFile, TrashDirectory, RestoreFile, PurgeFile and the legal-hold guard, and a grep across app/Livewire, app/Http, resources/views and routes found not one reference to any of them. Every one of those items was legitimately Complete against its own doneWhen; each had scoped itself to the action and its policy and deferred the UI, and five deferrals accumulated without anyone counting them, so the only way to trash a file in a shipped container was artisan tinker. The backlog could not express \"this page needs something to show\", which is also why item/trash-view was eligible to ship a Trash page that would have been empty in every real install.\n\nThe mutation harness rejected work I had already reviewed and approved, which is the most useful thing in this run. Seven of the eight new authorize calls are load-bearing by its own report. The eighth, browser-select-directory-view, kept passing with its guard deleted, because selecting a directory renders PropertyPanel and PropertyPanel::mount() authorises view on the same subject and refuses first: the 403 arrives either way and the test cannot tell which produced it. decision/0018 records the resolution as well as the finding, because they differ -- the authorize call stays, since PropertyPanel refusing is PropertyPanel's concern, but its manifest entry is deleted, since the entry claimed a proof that did not exist. CLAUDE.md's rule about a guard whose test passes without it is about false protection; here the protection was real and the proof was the fiction.\n\nThe rest of the run was a silent upload failure that cost nine rounds and taught decision/0019. The smoke had been reporting \"upload accepted, file listed in the directory\" on the strength of page.getByText(FILE_NAME), which matches the file input's own displayed filename -- so it announced success through an entire run in which File::where('name', ...) returned null for the next sixty seconds, and then failed somewhere else entirely, at extraction, behind a tinker error masked by PsySH's exit code. Four hypotheses about the cause were wrong in a row: an upload endpoint assumed about a Livewire version that cannot be inspected here, which on two runs became the failure itself; a window.Livewire wait that could not fail because the condition was already true after a wire:navigate swap; a page-sequence workaround pushed after I had already written that a refutation meant no more guessing; and the claim that two branches showing the symptom were one deterministic defect, when one of them did not reproduce. What settled it was instrumentation, three times over. waitForFileRow() turned \"extraction timed out\" into \"nothing was stored\" in one run. tinker() raising status, signal and stderr turned \"Command failed: <the command>\" into a readable cause. uploadAndProveStored() reported the answer outright: attempt 1 leaves no files row, attempt 2 stores it 2.5 seconds later. A timing window, on the same page, with no request made and no error raised -- and never the redirect's doing, though that branch wore the blame for nine of its ten rounds.\n\nTwo traps in the project's own conventions surfaced and are recorded where the next person will hit them. tests.yml runs on pull_request and pushes to main only, so the mutation procedure decision/0012 describes -- push a branch with the feature broken, read the image job -- produces no image job at all from a bare branch push, and silence reads like success; an earlier mutation branch of mine sat that way unnoticed. And a red mutation job is not evidence on its own: four notifications reported the TrashFile mutation had failed, and the run could equally have died at the upload race with the assertion never executing. The call log is what settles it, and it did: waiting for getByText('DoccumSmokeTrashTarget.txt') to be detached, 25 times resolved to visible.\n\nv1 is 20 of 42. Left open deliberately: issue 106, which stopped being a harness problem when the measurement came back, because a product that silently discards an upload issued within half a second of navigation -- no request, no error, and the listing still showing the name the input drew -- is a question about users rather than tests; and issue 109, an existence oracle in the move methods whose authorisation is sound, whose fix is written out, and which was not applied here because it converts a 403 into a 404 that the new tests assert and this environment cannot run them."
}
